WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.417
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
On this episode,

00:00:01.417 --> 00:00:03.305
we talk to doc.ai about
how large health care

00:00:03.305 --> 00:00:05.430
companies can start to gain
the benefits of running

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:06.427
in the cloud.

00:00:06.427 --> 00:00:08.255
[THEME MUSIC]

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:12.460
So how has the
cloud kind of been

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:15.208
a catalyst to making this change
in the industry as a whole?

00:00:15.208 --> 00:00:17.000
AKSHAY SHARMA: Yeah,
it's a great question.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:19.690
So if you look
at, traditionally,

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:23.800
most big health
care-related companies

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:25.300
are not really on the cloud.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:28.060
They're usually on
their own data centers.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:30.060
Moving to cloud has
many challenges,

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:32.800
so one is regulatory and
compliance challenge.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:35.020
We are seeing that most
cloud providers today

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:37.660
are HIPAA compliant out
of the box, which reduces

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:39.430
the sort of barrier to entry.

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:40.960
But it's still
pretty hard for them

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:44.260
to move massive amounts of
data to cloud because there

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:45.910
is cost associated with it.

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:47.950
But the stickiness
aspect with the cloud

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:51.110
is that you don't have to
build tools from the ground up,

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:53.920
because there are so many
services out of the box

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.980
that the cloud offers that it
makes valuable because it's

00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:58.930
a question of opportunity cost.

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:01.130
Can you get to market sooner?

00:01:01.130 --> 00:01:04.510
However, still there are enough
challenges for big health care

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:06.190
players to be on the cloud.

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:09.610
What we are seeing
at doc.ai startups--

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:12.010
which is a startup, basically--
we don't have that issue.

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:13.360
We can be on cloud.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:15.640
We are cloud-native from get-go.

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:20.140
So we want it to be
scalable, resilient, and use

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:23.590
all the services out
of the box so that we

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:25.180
can get to market sooner.

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:26.870
What we have seen is
we were able to get

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:28.750
to market significantly
faster, not

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:31.450
having to worry about
infrastructure or managing

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:34.480
servers, and use the
plethora of services

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:35.920
that Google Cloud provides.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:39.400
A great example of that is
Google Kubernetes Engine,

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:41.530
and along with all
the other containers

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:44.470
that we can easily roll out
and other type of services,

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:48.910
like SDL Proxy and so on, which
makes it really easy for us

00:01:48.910 --> 00:01:52.570
to not have a big infrastructure
and dev ops team, but focus

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:55.360
on rolling back
product features.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:57.840
So it's been great that
way to be on the cloud.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:58.390
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
And that makes sense

00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:00.640
for startups, who, you know,
they want to be a cloud native,

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:02.057
they need to get
to market sooner.

00:02:02.057 --> 00:02:04.320
It's really easy to be
agile when you're small.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.070
What would you say the
most important kind

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:08.740
of cloud technologies or
cloud-related resources

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:11.320
are available to larger
companies that can help them

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:13.360
adopt a similar methodology?

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:15.430
AKSHAY SHARMA: That's
a great question.

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:19.360
So I think it's a multi-phase
approach for bigger companies

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:21.470
that are not on cloud already.

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:25.750
So what I suggest is that any
of the new products and services

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:27.400
that you want to
roll out, you want

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:29.530
to consider being
on the cloud first.

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:32.530
So some principles
of being cloud-native

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:36.400
means that you can use some
of the open source technology

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:39.580
so that you're not necessarily
locked into one cloud vendor,

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:42.940
and you have the optionality
of being able to run that back

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:44.200
on your data center.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:47.140
So the first suggestion
from my point of view

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:50.830
is that you start building new
services and products as sort

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:53.570
of a cloud-native version
that can run on any cloud,

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:55.510
including your own data center.

00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:57.430
Second phase of
that would be, how

00:02:57.430 --> 00:03:00.100
do you sort of bring
other interesting data

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:02.800
that you sit on in
your own data centers

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:05.770
and use that in conjunction
with the data that's

00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:08.240
being collected on your
new services and products?

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:10.360
So you could bring your
own data to the cloud

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:14.110
or you could sink the data back
from the cloud to your own data

00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:17.650
centers, where you are now
building a much more robust set

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:19.420
of models or services.

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:21.610
But over time, as
phase three, I would

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:23.260
say that you may
want to consider

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:25.780
moving a lot of your
computational needs

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:26.770
onto the cloud.

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:30.130
The challenge with
that is at scale,

00:03:30.130 --> 00:03:32.600
cloud becomes pretty expensive.

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:34.570
An example of that
would be if you're

00:03:34.570 --> 00:03:36.280
building a lot of
interesting models

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:39.550
that require a lot of GPU
computational resources,

00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:42.440
then these are pretty
expensive on a per hour basis.

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:46.060
So it's still very valuable
to consider moving to a cloud,

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:48.010
but then build it and
design it in a way

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:50.722
that you're not tied
to a specific cloud.

00:03:50.722 --> 00:03:52.930
And there are many open
source tools and technologies

00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:54.395
that let you do
that so, that way,

00:03:54.395 --> 00:03:56.770
you can bring it back to your
own data center eventually.

00:03:56.770 --> 00:03:58.645
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah,
and a hybrid approach

00:03:58.645 --> 00:04:00.530
is still something that's
not only relatable,

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:02.572
but actually really
desirable to a lot of people.

00:04:02.572 --> 00:04:04.988
So how does that relate to the
cloud being more expensive?

00:04:04.988 --> 00:04:07.060
AKSHAY SHARMA: Right, so
it depends on where you

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:08.500
are in the company's journey.

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:11.560
So a big established
company may have already

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:14.930
owned the real estate for
having their own data centers.

00:04:14.930 --> 00:04:17.620
So the marginal cost
of adding the server

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:20.470
would still look far
more lucrative for them

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:23.590
to move to cloud, as
opposed to move to cloud.

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:26.560
But what I see is
that there is going

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:30.190
to be an explosion of
different tools and services,

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:33.122
and especially new type of
computation environments

00:04:33.122 --> 00:04:33.830
that you can get.

00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:37.330
For example, you're seeing
PPUs that Google is pioneering

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:38.860
and rolling out.

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:40.900
It'll almost be
impossible to bring those

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:43.130
to back to your data centers.

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:46.570
So in some way, you're
right that it's better

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:50.290
to be on cloud, because you
can manage costs certainly

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:53.120
in some way because you
only use it in on demand.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:56.830
But then with already at
scale, certain companies

00:04:56.830 --> 00:05:00.100
find it easier and cheaper
to roll out new machines,

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:03.550
but not necessarily offering
the same capabilities.

00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:08.670
It's an opportunity cost of
the future versus marginal cost

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:10.860
of the present day,
that these big companies

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:12.210
have to make a trade-off.

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:14.700
The initial cost will
be higher on the cloud,

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:18.390
simply because to replicate
the same level of computational

00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:20.800
resources, it's going
to be expensive.

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:24.885
But with the right sort of teams
and right infrastructure teams

00:05:24.885 --> 00:05:26.885
in place, where you're
not spending up resources

00:05:26.885 --> 00:05:29.370
and wasting it away,
you can somehow

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:30.900
have a handle on the cloud.

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:32.700
But I think really
it's the future

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:34.380
of where your
products can go, which

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:37.983
I think is only possible being
on a cloud provider today.

00:05:37.983 --> 00:05:40.650
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Stay tuned for
more conversations with doc.ai.

00:05:40.650 --> 00:05:42.942
If you enjoyed this episode,
check out the "Stack Chat"

00:05:42.942 --> 00:05:44.250
playlist for more great videos.

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:47.300
[CLOSING THEME]

